1
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:05,320
PROFESSOR: Hello, everyone,
and welcome to the content

2
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:08,820
video for graph traversal.

3
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:13,270
So we've talked about graph data
structure at an overview level.

4
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:17,190
We've talked about how do you
implement a graph as we go

5
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:20,260
from an idea to an application.

6
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:22,950
Now we're going to talk
about graph traversal, which

7
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:24,557
is I've got this big graph now.

8
00:00:24,557 --> 00:00:25,890
How do I get the data out of it?

9
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:28,620
And how do I do it fast, because
there's a lot of slow ways

10
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:29,930
to get through a graph?

11
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:31,640
How do we do the fastest way?

12
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:34,290
So graph traversal and
the graph data structure

13
00:00:34,290 --> 00:00:35,480
itself go hand in hand.

14
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:38,130
You need to analyze
the data structure

15
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:40,650
if you want to think about
what's the most efficient way

16
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:43,120
to traverse through the
graph to get data out of it.

17
00:00:48,036 --> 00:00:51,620
So really, there's two
standards for graph traversal,

18
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,800
depth-first search and
breadth-first search.

19
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,380
Depth-first search
means you go--

20
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:59,820
you-- let me start with
breadth-first, actually.

21
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:02,070
In my opinion, it's a little
bit easier to understand.

22
00:01:02,070 --> 00:01:04,727
So breadth-first is
I'm at a current node.

23
00:01:04,727 --> 00:01:06,560
What are all the nodes
I can go to from here

24
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:08,850
and analyze those
or visit those next?

25
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:11,480
So if I'm at node A and
I can get to B, C, D,

26
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:14,787
I go to B, then C,
then D in that order.

27
00:01:14,787 --> 00:01:16,620
So when I go to B, then
I've got the option,

28
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:18,470
well, I can go, come
back, and then go to C.

29
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:20,060
But I-- now that
I'm at B, I can just

30
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:22,185
keep going deeper and deeper
and deeper and deeper.

31
00:01:22,185 --> 00:01:23,720
And that's depth-first searches.

32
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,803
You go to the next node,
then go to the next node,

33
00:01:25,803 --> 00:01:28,280
then go to the next node,
then go to the next node

34
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:30,710
like that, whereas
breadth-first-- it's more you

35
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:33,230
keep track of where you're
at, where you've been,

36
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:38,650
and then you go to all
available nodes next together.

37
00:01:38,650 --> 00:01:41,970
Here's a fun real-life
application of depth-first--

38
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:44,450
of an issue that can
come up with depth-first.

39
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:45,750
It's a funny little cartoon.

40
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:48,420
But it's actually pretty
right on with the real issue

41
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:52,200
that comes up with-- when
you do a depth-first search.

42
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,106
So in this cartoon, the--

43
00:01:56,106 --> 00:02:00,570
this person, this guy, is
getting ready for a date.

44
00:02:00,570 --> 00:02:02,860
And what are the situations
I need to be prepared for?

45
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:04,860
So three situations--
think of those like nodes,

46
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:06,652
things I need to be
prepared for as nodes--

47
00:02:06,652 --> 00:02:08,979
medical emergency, dancing,
and food too expensive.

48
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:11,730
So then the next
slide or the next part

49
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:14,110
is, what kinds of
emergencies can happen?

50
00:02:14,110 --> 00:02:15,570
So that's the first node here.

51
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:17,820
Well, you go down there and
think snakebite, lightning

52
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,220
strike, fall from chair.

53
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:23,520
Well, then breadth-first would
have said, go back and then

54
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:24,850
go to dancing next here.

55
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:27,550
But depth-first just takes
you further down that hole.

56
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:31,320
That's the common turn when
your depth-first search starts

57
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:34,720
going into places
that gets off track.

58
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:37,920
So snakebite-- then go to corn
snake, garter snake, copperhead.

59
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:41,737
And then corn snake turns
into researching snake venoms.

60
00:02:41,737 --> 00:02:44,320
Research comparing snake venoms
is scattered and inconsistent.

61
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:45,880
I'll make a spreadsheet
to organize it.

62
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,860
And then on his date,
I'm here to pick you up.

63
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:48,690
You're not dressed.

64
00:02:48,690 --> 00:02:51,390
By LD50, the inland taipan
has the deadliest venom

65
00:02:51,390 --> 00:02:52,230
of any snake.

66
00:02:52,230 --> 00:02:56,100
So his brain went down the hole
of medical, snakebite, and then

67
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:58,260
the corn snake and
then venoms because he

68
00:02:58,260 --> 00:02:59,230
was going depth-first.

69
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:02,500
So what would a breadth-first
search look like?

70
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,010
You do medical emergencies
first, then dancing,

71
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:07,400
then food expensive.

72
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:09,088
Then you go deeper on medical.

73
00:03:09,088 --> 00:03:10,880
What kind of medical
emergency is on there?

74
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:13,450
Then the next layer of those
emergencies, you look at those.

75
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:15,610
Then you come back and say,
what kind of dancing issues

76
00:03:15,610 --> 00:03:16,160
could there be?

77
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:17,118
Then you look at those.

78
00:03:17,118 --> 00:03:18,460
That's breadth-first search.

79
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:21,010
And as a fun little side
note, the inland taipan--

80
00:03:21,010 --> 00:03:24,700
there's actually no human
deaths from the inland taipan

81
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:28,380
because they're very docile
and rarely, rarely bite.

82
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:31,090
But it is true that
that has the most venom.

83
00:03:31,090 --> 00:03:35,910
So graph traversal--
and then so DFS and BFS,

84
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:37,350
as you probably
may have guessed,

85
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:40,710
are depth-first search
and breadth-first search.

86
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:43,820
When doing these, it's--

87
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:45,840
the real difference
is keep track of nodes

88
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,100
that you've visited so far.

89
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:49,860
And that's a theme
with graph traversal--

90
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,110
is keeping track
of the nodes you've

91
00:03:52,110 --> 00:03:57,073
visited so far in a way
that's most efficient.

92
00:03:57,073 --> 00:03:58,240
I'm going to skip down here.

93
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:00,330
When a node x is visited,
it's labeled and visited

94
00:04:00,330 --> 00:04:01,330
and added to a tree.

95
00:04:01,330 --> 00:04:03,300
If a traversal got to
a node x from node y,

96
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:05,920
y is viewed as the parent
of x and x of a child.

97
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:07,500
So again, x and y,
the parent child--

98
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:10,740
they're-- it's all really set up
to keeping track of what nodes

99
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:14,060
we visited and what nodes
we need to visit next.

100
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:16,550
Depth-first search--
so here is the steps.

101
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:17,300
They're intuitive.

102
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:18,700
But we'll just go through
them really quick.

103
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:19,910
Select an unvisited node.

104
00:04:19,910 --> 00:04:21,920
Visit it and treat it
as the current node.

105
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:24,295
By the way, I'm going to throw
in a little reminder here.

106
00:04:24,295 --> 00:04:26,400
How did we traverse a tree?

107
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:27,050
Remember?

108
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:29,370
It was through recursion.

109
00:04:29,370 --> 00:04:31,160
So we called a
function like find,

110
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:35,540
where we gave the node we
want to go to current.left.

111
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:36,860
We call a function.

112
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:39,360
And current.left
becomes the new node.

113
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:42,240
So as we recurse down,
that's our new node.

114
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,490
That's the exact
same thing in graphs.

115
00:04:44,490 --> 00:04:45,720
Think of it recursively.

116
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:46,370
I'm at a node.

117
00:04:46,370 --> 00:04:47,690
I go to the next node.

118
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:49,460
And that's my new current node.

119
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:51,590
So that's an important
concept of the node

120
00:04:51,590 --> 00:04:55,130
I've been to and my current node
because current node changes.

121
00:04:55,130 --> 00:04:56,630
So we get to our
next current node,

122
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:58,730
then find an unvisited
neighbor of the current node,

123
00:04:58,730 --> 00:05:00,522
visit it, and make it
the new current node.

124
00:05:00,522 --> 00:05:02,610
We recurse down again to
the next current node.

125
00:05:02,610 --> 00:05:04,010
And 1-- maybe this
is a good time

126
00:05:04,010 --> 00:05:05,695
to bring up a
difference in trees.

127
00:05:05,695 --> 00:05:07,320
Trees are-- you can
keep track of them.

128
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:08,820
You're always going
down and back up

129
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:10,400
to visit left and
right children.

130
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,200
Well, in graphs,
that relationship

131
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:13,780
doesn't necessarily exist.

132
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:15,900
So now another thing
to keep track of

133
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:19,200
is all of the nodes you can
visit and whether or not

134
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:21,810
they've been visited,
like an adjacency list

135
00:05:21,810 --> 00:05:23,615
or an adjacency matrix.

136
00:05:23,615 --> 00:05:24,990
So then you would
just have a way

137
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:27,900
to mark those nodes as
visited or unvisited

138
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:31,470
in the adjacency matrix
or the adjacency list.

139
00:05:31,470 --> 00:05:34,420
And then backtrack refers
to same concept as a tree.

140
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:36,570
Once we visited, we have
to go back to the parent

141
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:38,620
to keep the traversal going.

142
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:41,740
Repeat steps 3 and 4 until
no more nodes can be found.

143
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:44,100
If there are still unvisited
nodes, repeat from step 1

144
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:45,040
and generate a forest.

145
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:46,770
So basically, just
keep recursing

146
00:05:46,770 --> 00:05:48,120
until you visited everything.

147
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:49,410
That's depth-first.

148
00:05:49,410 --> 00:05:51,750
Breadth-first versus
is very similar,

149
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:53,790
except now you keep
track of levels

150
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:55,260
that you've visited things.

151
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,640
Select an unvisited node x.

152
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:57,210
Visit it.

153
00:05:57,210 --> 00:05:59,770
Have it be the root in
a BFS tree being formed.

154
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:01,630
Its level is called
the current level.

155
00:06:01,630 --> 00:06:03,630
Try all in one-step
extensions of current paths

156
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:05,910
before trying larger extensions.

157
00:06:05,910 --> 00:06:08,640
The extensions refer-- try
one-step extension-- that's

158
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:10,630
like the medical emergency.

159
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:11,790
Just go one level down.

160
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:13,590
Go one and the medical
emergency and look

161
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:15,100
at different
medical emergencies.

162
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:17,775
Don't go all the way down
to inland taipan venom.

163
00:06:17,775 --> 00:06:19,650
Just go one step down
to medical emergencies.

164
00:06:19,650 --> 00:06:22,200
Then come back and
then look at dancing.

165
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,570
Then step 3-- repeat
step 2 until no more

166
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:25,620
nodes can be visited.

167
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:29,770
If there are still unvisited
nodes, repeat from step 1.

168
00:06:29,770 --> 00:06:31,700
That might have
sounded really wordy

169
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:33,780
and like, how am I going
to keep track of that?

170
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:37,050
It's actually pretty simple
if you make a tree out of it.

171
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:40,160
So all breadth-first
searches can

172
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:44,570
be turned into a tree, a tree
style of layers going down,

173
00:06:44,570 --> 00:06:46,450
not a binary search
tree because you can

174
00:06:46,450 --> 00:06:47,700
have more than one child here.

175
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:48,570
But take a look.

176
00:06:48,570 --> 00:06:51,020
So here's the graph we want
to traverse that I'm circling

177
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:52,500
with my mouse right now.

178
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:54,270
Here's the tree we
can make out of it.

179
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:55,173
So we start at 0.

180
00:06:55,173 --> 00:06:56,340
Well, where can I go from 0?

181
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:57,810
I can go to 1, 2, or 4.

182
00:06:57,810 --> 00:06:59,700
Think of it-- that's
my next level.

183
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:02,430
Well, where can I go to from 1?

184
00:07:02,430 --> 00:07:03,980
Well, 1-- I can get to 4.

185
00:07:03,980 --> 00:07:06,445
But I'm already-- 4 is
already in my visited list.

186
00:07:06,445 --> 00:07:07,820
It's already in
the level I'm at.

187
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:10,110
So I don't need to
keep going down there.

188
00:07:10,110 --> 00:07:12,950
2-- I've got new places
to go from there.

189
00:07:12,950 --> 00:07:15,765
4-- any place I can get to
from 4 I've already been to.

190
00:07:15,765 --> 00:07:17,390
So there's no need
to keep adding edges

191
00:07:17,390 --> 00:07:19,010
because I've
already visited it--

192
00:07:19,010 --> 00:07:21,090
again, that concept
of visited nodes.

193
00:07:21,090 --> 00:07:23,330
Then we go down from
here into node 2.

194
00:07:23,330 --> 00:07:25,560
Node 2-- I can get
to 5, 9, and 10.

195
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:27,480
Well, what new nodes
can I get to from there?

196
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:29,010
Well, from 5, I
can go to 6, 7, 8.

197
00:07:29,010 --> 00:07:30,240
9-- I can get to 11.

198
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:32,350
And from 2, I got to 10.

199
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:35,370
So breadth-first search--
and one of the pros of it

200
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:38,310
over depth-first search is it's
really nice to get a visual.

201
00:07:38,310 --> 00:07:41,100
And if you're working for a
social media company where

202
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:43,740
you have tens of
millions of users,

203
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:45,720
having a nice visual
of how to traverse

204
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:47,270
it can be really useful.

205
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:52,600
We talked about breadth-first
and depth-first.

206
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:53,590
Which one's faster?

207
00:07:53,590 --> 00:07:55,590
Well, it depends.

208
00:07:55,590 --> 00:07:58,110
And there's actually
multiple algorithms

209
00:07:58,110 --> 00:08:00,757
to traversing a graph.

210
00:08:00,757 --> 00:08:02,590
Obviously, everybody's
after the same thing.

211
00:08:02,590 --> 00:08:03,757
Which ones are the fastest?

212
00:08:03,757 --> 00:08:05,340
Well, in slightly
different scenarios,

213
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:08,010
slightly different
types of graphs.

214
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:11,160
But there are some algorithms
that just flat out win

215
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:12,810
at the fastest--

216
00:08:12,810 --> 00:08:14,190
shortest-path algorithm.

217
00:08:14,190 --> 00:08:16,320
So the shortest-path
algorithm I'll show you today

218
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:20,920
is the Dijkstra's algorithm.

219
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:23,830
So one thing we need to--

220
00:08:23,830 --> 00:08:26,240
one checkpoint we need
here is edge weights.

221
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:31,120
So if I think of an edge
weight like in a map,

222
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:33,010
let's say the graph
represents a map,

223
00:08:33,010 --> 00:08:34,730
an edge weight could
be the distance.

224
00:08:34,730 --> 00:08:38,770
So what's the shortest
distance I can drive

225
00:08:38,770 --> 00:08:40,978
from here to St. George, Utah?

226
00:08:40,978 --> 00:08:41,770
I'm in Logan, Utah.

227
00:08:41,770 --> 00:08:43,820
From Logan, Utah,
to St. George, Utah,

228
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:45,623
what's the shortest distance?

229
00:08:45,623 --> 00:08:47,540
Well, actually, I don't
know if you knew this.

230
00:08:47,540 --> 00:08:50,170
You can actually jump
from Brigham City 89

231
00:08:50,170 --> 00:08:53,070
and hit I-15 further south.

232
00:08:53,070 --> 00:08:54,070
It's a shorter distance.

233
00:08:54,070 --> 00:08:56,240
But it's less time on I-15.

234
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:58,870
So going straight to I-15 and
Brigham City and then down

235
00:08:58,870 --> 00:09:00,350
is actually way faster.

236
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:02,098
But how do we know that?

237
00:09:02,098 --> 00:09:03,640
Well, we know because
we probably all

238
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:05,932
tried it and people
have told their friends

239
00:09:05,932 --> 00:09:06,890
and everybody knows it.

240
00:09:06,890 --> 00:09:11,290
But what if you wanted
to definitively calculate

241
00:09:11,290 --> 00:09:12,920
what is the shortest path?

242
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:16,810
So the edge weight there
would be the time it takes.

243
00:09:16,810 --> 00:09:20,900
If-- or let's say if my goal is
to travel the shortest distance,

244
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:23,155
put the least amount of
miles on my car, that's

245
00:09:23,155 --> 00:09:25,280
a little different than I
want to get to St. George

246
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:26,370
as fast as possible.

247
00:09:26,370 --> 00:09:28,110
That's the least amount of time.

248
00:09:28,110 --> 00:09:32,720
So if all I'm interested is the
least amount of miles on my car,

249
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:34,220
my edge weights
I'm concerned with

250
00:09:34,220 --> 00:09:36,857
would be the distance of
the roads I'm driving on.

251
00:09:36,857 --> 00:09:38,690
If all I care about is
getting to St. George

252
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:41,500
as fast as possible, then
the time spent on the roads

253
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:42,500
would be my edge weight.

254
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:45,030
And that would be my
shortest-path algorithm.

255
00:09:45,030 --> 00:09:47,820
But same idea is I just need
to define the edge weights.

256
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:49,110
What does an edge weight mean?

257
00:09:49,110 --> 00:09:51,235
And one concept we're really
not going to get into,

258
00:09:51,235 --> 00:09:53,340
but it's good to be aware
of, is negative edges.

259
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:58,130
There are some graphs where
an edge has a positive value,

260
00:09:58,130 --> 00:09:59,720
like in our road example.

261
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:01,490
But there could also
be a negative edge,

262
00:10:01,490 --> 00:10:06,390
like calculating how much money
it takes to get from one place

263
00:10:06,390 --> 00:10:06,890
to another.

264
00:10:06,890 --> 00:10:09,920
It could be negative if you
have to go over a toll road.

265
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:12,780
Then that's an immediate loss of
money-- so something like that.

266
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:15,072
But we really won't get into
that negative edge weights

267
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:16,360
that much.

268
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:21,280
So Dijkstra's algorithm-- I'm
actually going to jump down to--

269
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:22,680
it's the same graph right here.

270
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:23,180
Here we go.

271
00:10:23,180 --> 00:10:24,910
So Dijkstra's algorithm--
we add the edge weight

272
00:10:24,910 --> 00:10:27,153
from the node to its
successor to the current node,

273
00:10:27,153 --> 00:10:29,320
all nodes from collection
based on shortest distance

274
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:30,940
using a priority queue.

275
00:10:30,940 --> 00:10:34,580
So Dijkstra's algorithm-- maybe
you've heard of it before.

276
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:36,320
It's actually really,
really simple.

277
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,320
And that's really
what, in my opinion--

278
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:39,640
what's so beautiful about it.

279
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:40,960
It's just easy-peasy.

280
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:44,118
And it will definitely
get you the shortest path.

281
00:10:44,118 --> 00:10:45,910
If you want to go from
one node to another,

282
00:10:45,910 --> 00:10:47,620
Dijkstra's will get
you the shortest path

283
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:51,280
and it-- faster than
other algorithms.

284
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:52,670
So here's the idea.

285
00:10:52,670 --> 00:10:55,600
We're going to-- so
V0 here is our vertice

286
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:57,290
or our node, same thing.

287
00:10:57,290 --> 00:10:58,808
And think of this like Logan.

288
00:10:58,808 --> 00:11:00,850
And I want to know, what's
the fastest way to get

289
00:11:00,850 --> 00:11:03,940
to all the other nodes in--

290
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:06,310
or all the other cities in Utah?

291
00:11:06,310 --> 00:11:09,530
From V0, what's the fastest
path to all of them?

292
00:11:09,530 --> 00:11:13,970
And Dijkstra's doesn't just
solve one-to-one, like V0 to V5.

293
00:11:13,970 --> 00:11:16,040
It'll solve V0 to everywhere.

294
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:18,980
What Dijkstra's does
not do-- it does not

295
00:11:18,980 --> 00:11:23,070
solve the shortest path from
everywhere to everywhere else.

296
00:11:23,070 --> 00:11:24,110
It's just one node.

297
00:11:24,110 --> 00:11:25,610
You've got to pick
a starting point.

298
00:11:25,610 --> 00:11:28,370
From this starting point,
how do I get from here

299
00:11:28,370 --> 00:11:30,690
to everywhere else the fastest?

300
00:11:30,690 --> 00:11:31,800
That's Dijkstra's.

301
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:33,950
So there's another
algorithm to solve

302
00:11:33,950 --> 00:11:36,450
how do I get from everywhere
to everywhere else the fastest.

303
00:11:36,450 --> 00:11:38,710
That's called
Ford-Fulkerson, which

304
00:11:38,710 --> 00:11:39,960
also handles negative weights.

305
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:41,950
So that's why
Ford-Fulkerson is popular.

306
00:11:41,950 --> 00:11:43,200
We're not going to cover that.

307
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:45,492
We're just going to cover
Dijkstra's because that one's

308
00:11:45,492 --> 00:11:48,950
probably the most commonly--

309
00:11:48,950 --> 00:11:51,420
one you should be aware of.

310
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:54,067
Dijkstra-- so
here's how it works.

311
00:11:54,067 --> 00:11:55,150
I'm going to draw it here.

312
00:11:55,150 --> 00:11:58,520
So from V0, here's
the rule we follow.

313
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:02,830
What node can I get to
that's currently unvisited

314
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:04,640
for the lowest amount?

315
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:07,070
We're going for the shortest
path-- so the shortest path.

316
00:12:07,070 --> 00:12:09,850
And so 1 would be my
shortest path here.

317
00:12:09,850 --> 00:12:12,290
From V0, what's the
shortest path I can get to?

318
00:12:12,290 --> 00:12:15,106
Well, I can get to V3 for--

319
00:12:15,106 --> 00:12:16,850
at a length of 1.

320
00:12:16,850 --> 00:12:19,100
I can get to V1
for a length of 2.

321
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:23,630
So my shortest path from V0
to V3 is this 1 right here.

322
00:12:23,630 --> 00:12:27,910
So I'm going to draw an arc next
to it to say that's visited.

323
00:12:27,910 --> 00:12:30,140
Now, here's the
beauty of Dijkstra's.

324
00:12:30,140 --> 00:12:34,390
Is it possible that I could
find a shorter path to V3

325
00:12:34,390 --> 00:12:36,290
later from V0?

326
00:12:36,290 --> 00:12:37,120
No.

327
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:38,150
How do you know that?

328
00:12:38,150 --> 00:12:40,750
Because even to get
to V1, I already

329
00:12:40,750 --> 00:12:44,860
have to pay a higher cost to get
to V1 than it was to get to V3.

330
00:12:44,860 --> 00:12:47,060
I got to go to cost
of 2 right there.

331
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:53,380
So even if I found an edge later
that was 0.000001 to get to V3,

332
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:55,040
I've already gone of length 2.

333
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:56,870
I already have a cost of 2.

334
00:12:56,870 --> 00:12:59,380
So no matter what I find
later to get me to V3,

335
00:12:59,380 --> 00:13:04,050
it'll never be
lower than V0 to V3.

336
00:13:04,050 --> 00:13:06,360
And that's really--
that's the simple beauty.

337
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:10,370
I take the shortest path I
have to an unvisited node.

338
00:13:10,370 --> 00:13:13,040
And I know I won't find
a shorter path later

339
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:15,860
by going to another way because
that other way is already

340
00:13:15,860 --> 00:13:17,030
more expensive.

341
00:13:17,030 --> 00:13:18,720
And then you just do
that over and over.

342
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:20,970
So from V0, I got to V3.

343
00:13:20,970 --> 00:13:23,150
Well, for my two
visited nodes now,

344
00:13:23,150 --> 00:13:25,190
what are the next
unvisited nodes

345
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:26,960
I can get to that have
the shortest path?

346
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:28,895
Well, V0 and V3--

347
00:13:28,895 --> 00:13:31,430
I still can get
to V1 for cost 2.

348
00:13:31,430 --> 00:13:33,530
I can get to V4 for a cost
of 3 because, remember,

349
00:13:33,530 --> 00:13:36,680
I have to go through
V3, then to V4.

350
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:40,530
I can go to V6 by a cost of 5.

351
00:13:40,530 --> 00:13:42,480
I can get to V5 by a cost of 9.

352
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:44,760
I can get to V2 by a cost of 3.

353
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:47,720
So the shortest path or the
lowest cost I can take right now

354
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:50,910
is V0 to V1 at a cost of 2.

355
00:13:50,910 --> 00:13:55,010
And that's the
next node I visit.

356
00:13:55,010 --> 00:13:56,747
And then we just do
it-- we just repeat

357
00:13:56,747 --> 00:13:57,830
until we visit every node.

358
00:13:57,830 --> 00:13:58,640
And that's it.

359
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:00,290
So from these three
visited nodes,

360
00:14:00,290 --> 00:14:03,590
what's the next unvisited node I
can get to for the lowest cost,

361
00:14:03,590 --> 00:14:04,960
for the shortest path?

362
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:08,310
Well, I can get, again,
to V2 for a cost of 3,

363
00:14:08,310 --> 00:14:09,780
V4 for a cost of 3.

364
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:11,850
I can go this way
for a cost of 12.

365
00:14:11,850 --> 00:14:14,280
I can go back to
V5 for a cost of 9.

366
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:16,430
I can get to V6 for a cost of 5.

367
00:14:16,430 --> 00:14:18,800
And the shortest-- you
can see it's a tie--

368
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:22,340
is I can get to V2
for a cost of 3.

369
00:14:22,340 --> 00:14:24,080
And I'll also do--

370
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:26,000
I'll just do the next
one right now also.

371
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,170
I can get to V4 for a cost of 3.

372
00:14:28,170 --> 00:14:29,990
So now I visited
five of the nodes.

373
00:14:29,990 --> 00:14:35,540
And the shortest cost-- the
largest cost I have so far is 3.

374
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:36,960
What's the next
one I can get to?

375
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:39,170
Well, from my currently
visited nodes-- and again,

376
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:40,620
it's all from V0.

377
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:43,260
Remember, when I calculate
path, it's from V0.

378
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:46,730
It's not I'm at V3, which is
independent of this whole thing.

379
00:14:46,730 --> 00:14:48,680
V3 is a visited node.

380
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:52,380
That's part of my path from V0.

381
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:53,600
So then I do it again.

382
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:58,210
V0-- I can get to V6 for
a cost of 5 right here.

383
00:14:58,210 --> 00:15:01,740
I can get to V6 if I go
this way for a cost of 9.

384
00:15:01,740 --> 00:15:05,190
I can get to V5 still
for a cost of 9 this way.

385
00:15:05,190 --> 00:15:08,520
I can get to V5 coming
this way from V3 to V2

386
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:11,440
and then V5 for a cost of 7.

387
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:13,620
So of all the ones available,
I know the next one

388
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:16,820
is V6 at a cost of 5.

389
00:15:16,820 --> 00:15:18,230
I visit that edge.

390
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:20,060
And now that I'm
there, well, remember,

391
00:15:20,060 --> 00:15:21,230
I haven't gotten to V5 yet.

392
00:15:21,230 --> 00:15:24,650
But this edge-- this path I've
looked the whole time of cost 9,

393
00:15:24,650 --> 00:15:25,730
9, 9--

394
00:15:25,730 --> 00:15:28,290
well, now that I've gotten to
V6, I've got a cheaper option.

395
00:15:28,290 --> 00:15:32,853
I can go V0 to V3 to V6
to V5 for a cost of 6.

396
00:15:32,853 --> 00:15:33,770
I can come over here--

397
00:15:33,770 --> 00:15:36,830
1, 2, 5-- for cost of 7
or my original one that's

398
00:15:36,830 --> 00:15:38,780
been available the
whole time, a cost of 9.

399
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:45,750
And the cheapest one is
this path for a cost of 6.

400
00:15:45,750 --> 00:15:47,170
All nodes have been visited.

401
00:15:47,170 --> 00:15:47,760
I'm done.

402
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:51,370
Now I've given a path from
V0 to every other node.

403
00:15:51,370 --> 00:15:53,380
That's the shortest path.

404
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,540
Dijkstra's is used
by Google Maps.

405
00:15:55,540 --> 00:15:58,300
To be honest, it
uses variations.

406
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:00,830
But that's how it does it.

407
00:16:00,830 --> 00:16:03,460
It figures out all the shortest
paths to the adjacent nodes

408
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:06,790
and eventually on to St. George.

409
00:16:06,790 --> 00:16:09,310
So if you're thinking,
well, St. George--

410
00:16:09,310 --> 00:16:11,350
do you really need to
visit all of the nodes

411
00:16:11,350 --> 00:16:13,960
from Logan to St. George
to figure out the shortest

412
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:14,990
path to St. George?

413
00:16:14,990 --> 00:16:15,490
No.

414
00:16:15,490 --> 00:16:17,500
Again, Google Maps
uses variations.

415
00:16:17,500 --> 00:16:20,890
But also remember, what
if there's an I-15 here

416
00:16:20,890 --> 00:16:25,520
that's cost of 6.5?

417
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:28,900
Well, I would find it after
I'm visiting all these nodes

418
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:29,780
to St. George.

419
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:33,020
Then I'm like, that one--
let's call it the I-15 edge.

420
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:34,638
That one is cheaper
than everything.

421
00:16:34,638 --> 00:16:36,430
I could guarantee it's
cheaper because I've

422
00:16:36,430 --> 00:16:37,420
looked at other ways.

423
00:16:37,420 --> 00:16:39,260
And that one is
the shortest path.

424
00:16:39,260 --> 00:16:45,020
So that's the simplicity and
the beauty of Dijkstra's.

425
00:16:47,730 --> 00:16:50,777
And then a quick
review-- so min spanning

426
00:16:50,777 --> 00:16:53,110
trees-- we talked about trees
with breadth-first search.

427
00:16:53,110 --> 00:16:56,670
We won't review
min spanning trees.

428
00:16:56,670 --> 00:17:03,180
But I should add, though, there
are a lot of powerful Python

429
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:05,250
libraries that we use.

430
00:17:05,250 --> 00:17:06,930
So you are not
going to be required

431
00:17:06,930 --> 00:17:10,363
to code the traversal
algorithms themselves.

432
00:17:10,363 --> 00:17:12,780
But you still need to be aware
of the concepts of visited,

433
00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:16,420
unvisited, and how it works
and how it gives it its speed.

434
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:18,720
So when you do go to use
these Python libraries

435
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:20,520
that I'm going to give you
and show you how to use--

436
00:17:20,520 --> 00:17:23,187
that you understand what they're
doing and when to use which one

437
00:17:23,187 --> 00:17:24,910
and why one might be the best.

438
00:17:24,910 --> 00:17:28,113
So that'll be it.

439
00:17:28,113 --> 00:17:29,530
That concludes
this content video.

440
00:17:29,530 --> 00:17:31,320
Thanks for watching.